'' 'procptr( f, sub( ) )' PICKS AN OVERLOAD BY SIGNATURE. Measured, over two subs
'' called 's' and two functions called 'g':
''
''   procptr( s, sub( ) )                &_Z1Sv
''   procptr( s, sub( byval as byte ) )  &_Z1Sa
''
'' -- the second argument is a procedure TYPE and it names which of them is meant.
'' Only the VIRTUAL form of the two-argument spelling was read for a bare name, so
'' every one of these declined; the MEMBER form read it but could not match, and took
'' the first overload instead -- a silently wrong function address.
''
'' TWO THINGS HAD TO BE FIXED TOGETHER. 'sub( )' in an ordinary call argument is
'' neither a lambda -- a lambda has a body -- nor anything else the expression parser
'' reads, so it came out as an EMPTY typeref that matched nothing: procptr needs its
'' own argument parser, taking a TYPE second when it starts with SUB or FUNCTION and
'' an expression otherwise, which is what 'procptr( T.m, VIRTUAL )' needs.
''
'' And a SUB spells its absent result TWO WAYS: a declared one carries TYPE_NONE and a
'' procedure TYPE carries the VOID builtin, because ResolveTypeRef fills in what a
'' declaration leaves empty. Comparing them raw made every 'procptr( s, sub( ) )' miss
'' its own overload.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/procptr-signature.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: func.addr ptr _Z1Sv
'' EXPECT-OUT: func.addr ptr _Z1Sa
'' GOLDEN: expected/procptr-signature.out
